#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172710} :activity/id #uuid "f583bddf-73b7-5f01-9e8b-d128922ac57a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050369 :topic/id #uuid "43d73371-7529-529d-b89f-9e63a2ec6eaf" :topic/math-academy-id 3412} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173297 :tutorial/content "To **disprove** a statement means to show that it is false. In this lesson, we will learn how to disprove universal statements.\n\nSuppose we wish to disprove a universal statement of the form \n\n$$\n\\forall x \\in U, \\: P(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is some universal set. To do that, we must demonstrate that the statement's negation is true. \n\nThe negation of this statement is\n\n$$\n\\neg \\big( \\forall x \\in U, \\: P(x) \\big) \\: \\equiv \\: \\exists x \\in U, \\: \\lnot P(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, to disprove a universal statement, finding one value of the variable that makes the negation true is sufficient. This value is called a **counterexample** to the original statement." :tutorial/id #uuid "ac5efd40-5d78-5a92-a14d-9b292d2cf5dc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9356 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4b516377-68a8-5a1c-aae3-dfee168a79c0" :step/math-academy-id 162326 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173275 :tutorial/content "For example, suppose we wish to disprove the following statement:\n\n$5n-1$ is odd for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ \n\nOur statement can be written using formal notation as follows:\n![](images/d2/d22dcb4aa35870e9df2417316f7de327000f9b0cbd6758f79d4552d2a144cde1.svg)\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n![](images/32/3290200527bb59151b38e031c2b4a501784ccd6ca333bc7bef22ce5096ac6fce.svg)\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $5n-1$ is even.*\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=1$ suffices:\n\n*Notice that by substituting $n=1$ into the expression, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n-1 & =5(1)-1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*which is even.*\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\n*So, $n=1$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's write down the full argument." :tutorial/id #uuid "8199cce1-5561-58e7-98fa-8cdf64988b6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8346 :tutorial/title "Disproving a Statement About Parity"} :step/id #uuid "1a41cb7b-17d2-514f-85f3-6ccc085187b0" :step/math-academy-id 162327 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173298 :tutorial/content "Disprove the following statement:\n\n*$5n-1$ is odd for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$*\n\nCounterexample:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $5n-1$ is even.*\n\n*Notice that by substituting $n=1$ into the expression, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n-1 & =5(1)-1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*which is even.*\n\n*So, $n=1$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe1dd960-2b39-501f-a632-d369a200c00c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9357 :tutorial/title "Stating the Full Counterexample"} :step/id #uuid "98938657-bf5e-553d-b24d-4dedcdac91cd" :step/math-academy-id 162328 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171736 :question/id #uuid "525511f3-67d1-56e3-93ab-acd4e9801e96" :question/math-academy-id "e-13352"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18ffc9f1-9524-50c2-8ae8-e01236f453b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049552}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409537}] :knowledge-point/title "Stating Counterexample Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "315e0582-43af-5b99-8cba-1ad7e0fa3840" :step/math-academy-id 162329 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171733 :question/id #uuid "753bc7ba-2d9a-598a-8b39-19d4292b6cdc" :question/math-academy-id "e-13338"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fffe685-2f1e-5f0c-ab6a-db5786f40143" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172422}] :knowledge-point/title "Disproving Parity and Divisibility Statements"} :step/id #uuid "4f4006d0-bd46-5a8f-badf-2fa31f883637" :step/math-academy-id 162332 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172715}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171931 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171737 :question/id #uuid "331f2d85-cde0-5d83-abce-910d845845f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13353"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e48ea1-2219-5954-8101-def7c9c2be2c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172453}] :knowledge-point/title "Disproving Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "9843e5bd-147a-5718-bf2f-2cd9510ea7e2" :step/math-academy-id 162335}] :activity/title "Disproving Universal Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172195}]}
